00:01:34.210 --> 00:01:37.210
So I'd love to start by
talking about our perspective

00:01:37.210 --> 00:01:37.710
on hybrid.

00:01:37.710 --> 00:01:40.560
Hybrid's a pretty
heavily overloaded term

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:43.260
in the industry in general.

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:44.790
There's really no
single definition

00:01:44.790 --> 00:01:45.510
that really captures it.

00:01:45.510 --> 00:01:46.320
It's pretty broad.

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:48.210
When you look at
the dictionary, it's

00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:52.260
really the two distinct things
that are blended together

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:54.270
in some odd way, that's hybrid.

00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:56.716
So almost anything
could qualify as hybrid.

00:01:56.716 --> 00:01:58.590
I'm going to talk about
how we feel about it.

00:01:58.590 --> 00:02:00.930
And how we see Google
Platform contributing

00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:03.420
to its further development.

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:05.010
But first, it's
important to realize

00:02:05.010 --> 00:02:07.380
that hybrid is a reality.

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:10.000
I've ran into customers
in the industry--

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:12.880
I've been working in the cloud
for actually over 10 years,

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:15.630
so not always with Google,
with others as well.

00:02:15.630 --> 00:02:20.490
And it is an industry
term that gets used a lot.

00:02:20.490 --> 00:02:22.947
And often it's, well, hybrid
is not necessarily clean.

00:02:22.947 --> 00:02:24.030
It's not necessarily pure.

00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:26.880
But it's actually a reality that
most people are dealing with.

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:30.750
This survey you see
here, 87% of enterprises

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:34.590
are looking at a hybrid
strategy, have some form of it.

00:02:34.590 --> 00:02:37.200
56% are already using
multiple types of deployments,

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:39.010
multiple clouds.

00:02:39.010 --> 00:02:43.290
And only 40% are really
capturing cloud first

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:44.586
as a model.

00:02:44.586 --> 00:02:46.710
And even the folks that
are doing cloud first often

00:02:46.710 --> 00:02:48.190
have to go through
some transition.

00:02:48.190 --> 00:02:49.900
Hybrid is a transitory
phase as well.

00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:52.441
So you're going to probably run
into this unless you've never

00:02:52.441 --> 00:02:53.550
built any software.

00:02:53.550 --> 00:02:56.320
Who here has no software to run?

00:02:56.320 --> 00:02:56.920
Excellent.

00:02:56.920 --> 00:02:57.830
OK, good.

00:02:57.830 --> 00:02:58.810
I mean, if you have
no software to run,

00:02:58.810 --> 00:03:00.730
you probably have other
talks you want to go to.

00:03:00.730 --> 00:03:02.200
This is about people
that have software.

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:03.100
They have to move it.

00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:05.110
And how do you do
that in a way that's

00:03:05.110 --> 00:03:08.090
efficient and makes sense?

00:03:08.090 --> 00:03:11.650
So with that, hybrid is
persistent, and it's pragmatic.

00:03:11.650 --> 00:03:13.300
One of the ways to
look at hybrid--

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:15.330
and I like to misuse terms.

00:03:15.330 --> 00:03:17.500
So I like to tap in other
scientific disciplines

00:03:17.500 --> 00:03:19.300
like thermodynamics.

00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:21.874
And entropy is a
common term around how

00:03:21.874 --> 00:03:23.290
things when left
alone, eventually

00:03:23.290 --> 00:03:25.420
devolve into a more
chaotic state or maybe

00:03:25.420 --> 00:03:27.290
a more natural chaos.

00:03:27.290 --> 00:03:29.290
And hybrid really in many
ways is the expression

00:03:29.290 --> 00:03:30.940
of that in the IT landscape.

00:03:30.940 --> 00:03:33.530
As your platform evolves,
as you add new services,

00:03:33.530 --> 00:03:35.290
new capabilities, as
you add extensions

00:03:35.290 --> 00:03:37.090
to your architecture,
you oftentimes

00:03:37.090 --> 00:03:38.890
end up in a blend
of technologies,

00:03:38.890 --> 00:03:40.952
a blend of environments,
a blend of services.

00:03:40.952 --> 00:03:42.910
And so this is something
that naturally occurs.

00:03:42.910 --> 00:03:45.290
But that's not
necessarily bad news.

00:03:45.290 --> 00:03:49.610
Hybrid, in fact, is actually
desired for many reasons.

00:03:49.610 --> 00:03:54.160
So enterprises, in general,
often never single-source IT.

00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:55.840
That's common knowledge.

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:57.760
Don't put all your
eggs in one basket.

00:03:57.760 --> 00:03:59.860
So a hybrid strategy
is inherent if you're

00:03:59.860 --> 00:04:03.290
going to use multiple clouds,
multiple environments.

00:04:03.290 --> 00:04:05.770
The other thing is you
get some advantages

00:04:05.770 --> 00:04:08.440
of extending your environment
with the best of breed.

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:10.370
So hybrid is an
opportunity to say, hey,

00:04:10.370 --> 00:04:12.204
I've got the best pieces
from this platform.

00:04:12.204 --> 00:04:13.786
And I want to blend
them with the best

00:04:13.786 --> 00:04:15.124
pieces from that platform.

00:04:15.124 --> 00:04:16.540
And that functionally
is a hybrid.

00:04:16.540 --> 00:04:18.623
So there's some benefits
to that approach as well.

00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:23.710
And then fundamentally
embracing hybrid,

00:04:23.710 --> 00:04:26.990
which I think is what I'm
trying to encourage folks to do,

00:04:26.990 --> 00:04:30.400
influences how you think
about IT and how you wield it.

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:34.290
So instead of thinking of every
transition or every migration

00:04:34.290 --> 00:04:37.830
as a I'm going from A to
B, and B is my final state,

00:04:37.830 --> 00:04:40.680
you recognize that hybrid will
continue to exist over time.

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:44.250
That means you adopt approaches
that are not about n plus 1,

00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:45.845
but what's beyond that?

00:04:45.845 --> 00:04:47.970
So if you're going from A
to B, what's C look like?

00:04:47.970 --> 00:04:49.345
What are the tools
and techniques

00:04:49.345 --> 00:04:51.420
I'm going to want to
use going forward even

00:04:51.420 --> 00:04:52.860
beyond this current transition?

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:55.230
How do I think about
capturing and choosing

00:04:55.230 --> 00:04:57.840
tools that are going
to benefit me over time

00:04:57.840 --> 00:04:59.800
and not just for
this one period?

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:03.150
So thinking about that approach
differently changes perhaps

00:05:03.150 --> 00:05:05.070
how you think about the
partners you select,

00:05:05.070 --> 00:05:09.670
the tools you choose, and
the approaches you take.

00:05:09.670 --> 00:05:12.550
So all in mass migrations,
people talk about them.

00:05:12.550 --> 00:05:13.520
They're fantastic.

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:14.530
It's a great idea.

00:05:14.530 --> 00:05:16.870
But even with that, over
time, the technology landscape

00:05:16.870 --> 00:05:17.432
will change.

00:05:17.432 --> 00:05:19.390
You will end up probably
in some form of hybrid

00:05:19.390 --> 00:05:21.920
again later after
that mass migration.

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:23.500
So thinking about
how to tackle that

00:05:23.500 --> 00:05:26.860
in a efficient long-term
way is definitely

00:05:26.860 --> 00:05:29.407
beneficial to you
and your migrations.

00:05:29.407 --> 00:05:30.490
So it comes in many forms.

00:05:30.490 --> 00:05:32.324
As you can see here,
we have on the bottom--

00:05:32.324 --> 00:05:34.948
well, let me see here, I've got
your left-- there's the bottom.

00:05:34.948 --> 00:05:35.500
There you go.

00:05:35.500 --> 00:05:38.710
We have an open-source-y,
cloudy type tools here,

00:05:38.710 --> 00:05:41.530
and my data center or
more traditional bits,

00:05:41.530 --> 00:05:43.547
some other cloud that's
not GCP, and, of course,

00:05:43.547 --> 00:05:44.380
the Google Platform.

00:05:44.380 --> 00:05:47.440
So a hybrid could be, I'm
blending two data centers

00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:49.150
with disparate technologies.

00:05:49.150 --> 00:05:52.630
Not as exciting for this talk,
but that's a form of hybrid.

00:05:52.630 --> 00:05:55.630
Or I'm connecting my
traditional with a whole bunch

00:05:55.630 --> 00:05:59.320
of cloud first or
cloud-native services.

00:05:59.320 --> 00:06:01.480
Alternatively, I'm
using a common set

00:06:01.480 --> 00:06:03.310
of tools, a common set
of open-source tools

00:06:03.310 --> 00:06:04.518
across multiple environments.

00:06:04.518 --> 00:06:07.960
Here you see Kubernetes
down below, GKE up above,

00:06:07.960 --> 00:06:11.120
TensorFlow down below, Machine
Learning Engine up above.

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:13.120
So that's another blend
of hybrid technologies.

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:17.350
And of course, I would in my
definition of hybrid point

00:06:17.350 --> 00:06:19.852
to multicloud as a
hybrid mental model

00:06:19.852 --> 00:06:22.060
because you're actually
blending different approaches

00:06:22.060 --> 00:06:24.110
and different
technologies across that.

00:06:24.110 --> 00:06:26.920
So all of these really
are hybrid of a form.

00:06:26.920 --> 00:06:28.960
And so there may be things
that you'll run into.

00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:30.550
And they're all
valid and reasonable

00:06:30.550 --> 00:06:33.460
depending on what
makes sense for you.

00:06:33.460 --> 00:06:35.140
And then I'd like to
highlight that one.

00:06:35.140 --> 00:06:37.780
And hybrid is, of
course, at every layer.

00:06:37.780 --> 00:06:41.860
Thinking about hybrid, it's
not a particular architecture

00:06:41.860 --> 00:06:42.870
inherently.

00:06:42.870 --> 00:06:44.330
It generally is a
blend of things.

00:06:44.330 --> 00:06:45.280
And so it actually fits.

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:46.655
And there's tools
that are needed

00:06:46.655 --> 00:06:48.250
across the board at every layer.

00:06:48.250 --> 00:06:51.386
Fundamental to the whole thing
is, obviously, networking.

00:06:51.386 --> 00:06:53.260
There's no hybrid
architecture that I've ever

00:06:53.260 --> 00:06:55.040
seen that doesn't
have a network in it.

00:06:55.040 --> 00:06:57.167
So kind of makes sense.

00:06:57.167 --> 00:06:58.750
So you have to have
a series of tools.

00:06:58.750 --> 00:07:01.540
And you have to have a rich
platform and technology that

00:07:01.540 --> 00:07:05.800
allows you to extend your
network as it stands today,

00:07:05.800 --> 00:07:07.810
integrate other
services, and then build

00:07:07.810 --> 00:07:09.790
a dynamic network
on the other end

00:07:09.790 --> 00:07:12.070
to support your
hybrid migration.

00:07:12.070 --> 00:07:15.850
So in GCP, we have a variety of
different tools from Dedicated

00:07:15.850 --> 00:07:18.850
and Partner Interconnect for
bridging that gap as well

00:07:18.850 --> 00:07:21.460
as, obviously VPNs,
and then things

00:07:21.460 --> 00:07:24.340
like virtual private cloud, load
balancing, Cloud VPN as ways

00:07:24.340 --> 00:07:27.070
to build out that
network on the cloud.

00:07:27.070 --> 00:07:29.170
And that maps back to
your traditional routers

00:07:29.170 --> 00:07:31.310
and switches that
you might own today.

00:07:31.310 --> 00:07:33.670
At the next layer up,
you have infrastructure

00:07:33.670 --> 00:07:36.430
as a service or tech capability.

00:07:36.430 --> 00:07:39.460
So traditionally over here,
you've got your old VMs.

00:07:39.460 --> 00:07:41.650
You've got your persistent
disk you might have.

00:07:41.650 --> 00:07:44.200
This looks like a very
traditional, legacy,

00:07:44.200 --> 00:07:47.330
virtualized infrastructure
in the enterprise,

00:07:47.330 --> 00:07:49.960
and a series of tools like
the transfer appliance,

00:07:49.960 --> 00:07:53.890
or even our new Velostrata
tools that actually allow

00:07:53.890 --> 00:07:55.610
you to migrate VMs in minutes.

00:07:55.610 --> 00:07:59.350
So that's how you get those
existing lift-and-shift models

00:07:59.350 --> 00:08:01.010
to move those components over.

00:08:01.010 --> 00:08:04.810
And on the GCP side, you've got
Compute Engine, Cloud Storage,

00:08:04.810 --> 00:08:07.660
even Cloud Spanner to replace
maybe some traditional database

00:08:07.660 --> 00:08:10.030
technologies.

00:08:10.030 --> 00:08:12.420
Up top, the next layer of
hybrid is thinking about it

00:08:12.420 --> 00:08:14.027
in the application approach.

00:08:14.027 --> 00:08:15.610
So in the infrastructure
layer, you're

00:08:15.610 --> 00:08:17.890
maybe blending some
different types

00:08:17.890 --> 00:08:19.324
of services or components.

00:08:19.324 --> 00:08:20.990
At the application
layer, you're saying,

00:08:20.990 --> 00:08:22.531
look, I'm going to
pick a common tool

00:08:22.531 --> 00:08:24.760
chain, a common set of
services, like Kubernetes,

00:08:24.760 --> 00:08:26.682
across the board,
Istio across the board.

00:08:26.682 --> 00:08:28.640
And I'm going to run that
in both environments.

00:08:28.640 --> 00:08:32.320
So I can actually have a
single software development

00:08:32.320 --> 00:08:34.450
stack everywhere.

00:08:34.450 --> 00:08:37.179
And not everyone goes that
way from a hybrid perspective

00:08:37.179 --> 00:08:39.490
because maybe they're
not in containers today.

00:08:39.490 --> 00:08:43.690
But this is a fundamental model
that you see with our Cloud

00:08:43.690 --> 00:08:45.220
Service Platform announcement.

00:08:45.220 --> 00:08:47.890
We're espousing this idea of a
common tool chain everywhere.

00:08:47.890 --> 00:08:50.340
But it does require
possibly some rewrite.

00:08:50.340 --> 00:08:51.930
But that is
something to consider

00:08:51.930 --> 00:08:53.850
is how do you get to that layer?

00:08:53.850 --> 00:08:55.870
And that's a modernization
step generally

00:08:55.870 --> 00:08:57.964
for existing applications.

00:08:57.964 --> 00:09:00.380
And then fundamentally on top,
what I think a lot of folks

00:09:00.380 --> 00:09:01.755
forget about in
the hybrid model,

00:09:01.755 --> 00:09:05.570
is this idea of a common
set of services, API

00:09:05.570 --> 00:09:09.380
management, visibility in terms
of monitoring across the board.

00:09:09.380 --> 00:09:12.380
As you add the services
and capabilities

00:09:12.380 --> 00:09:15.110
in multiple environments,
the management infrastructure

00:09:15.110 --> 00:09:17.650
gets even more complicated,
so thinking about a way

00:09:17.650 --> 00:09:20.600
to pick tools and technologies
that give you visibility

00:09:20.600 --> 00:09:22.250
across the board,
like, for example,

00:09:22.250 --> 00:09:25.820
Stackdriver which can work
across multiple clouds,

00:09:25.820 --> 00:09:28.040
as well as even your
own platform or Apigee,

00:09:28.040 --> 00:09:30.510
which can work across
multiple environments as well.

00:09:30.510 --> 00:09:32.300
So those are
definitely key elements

00:09:32.300 --> 00:09:35.674
to consider across the board.

00:09:35.674 --> 00:09:37.090
Now our approach
to hybrid is what

00:09:37.090 --> 00:09:39.850
I'd like to talk about next,
but after a drink of water.

00:09:43.800 --> 00:09:45.010
I talked about the network.

00:09:45.010 --> 00:09:46.110
The network is the foundation.

00:09:46.110 --> 00:09:47.526
And without the
network, you don't

00:09:47.526 --> 00:09:48.870
have a strong hybrid story.

00:09:48.870 --> 00:09:51.990
And ideally, to make a lot
of these architectures work,

00:09:51.990 --> 00:09:55.380
you have to have a rich,
global performant network.

00:09:55.380 --> 00:09:58.470
And that's what Google's been
building for 18-plus years.

00:09:58.470 --> 00:10:01.770
We have 17 regions,
125 points of presence.

00:10:01.770 --> 00:10:03.960
We have over 100,000
feet of undersea cable

00:10:03.960 --> 00:10:05.520
that we've invested in.

00:10:05.520 --> 00:10:08.166
This is the largest private
network in the world.

00:10:08.166 --> 00:10:09.540
And we continue
to build that up.

00:10:09.540 --> 00:10:11.700
This is our continued
ongoing commitment.

00:10:11.700 --> 00:10:14.250
This means that you
have access, when

00:10:14.250 --> 00:10:16.320
you use GCP with your
hybrid environment,

00:10:16.320 --> 00:10:17.400
to this entire network.

00:10:17.400 --> 00:10:20.640
And as your data flows between
regions, between endpoints,

00:10:20.640 --> 00:10:22.650
you have that
backbone that we've

00:10:22.650 --> 00:10:24.120
built for our own platform.

00:10:24.120 --> 00:10:26.880
And you can actually ride
on top of that world-class

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:29.040
infrastructure.

00:10:29.040 --> 00:10:31.800
And we like to make it easy for
you to get involved in that.

00:10:31.800 --> 00:10:34.530
So we obviously have
the Google network.

00:10:34.530 --> 00:10:36.780
But we also build
multiple ways to get in.

00:10:36.780 --> 00:10:40.440
There's premium versus standard,
different network approaches.

00:10:40.440 --> 00:10:42.930
You can just do
VPN tunnels if you

00:10:42.930 --> 00:10:44.580
want something quick and easy.

00:10:44.580 --> 00:10:47.454
But we also have Dedicated
Interconnects or Partner

00:10:47.454 --> 00:10:49.620
Interconnects where you can
drop in to a data center

00:10:49.620 --> 00:10:53.670
that you're already in and
drop a simple 10-gig connection

00:10:53.670 --> 00:10:56.790
across the data center to tap
right into the Google network.

00:10:56.790 --> 00:10:59.040
So giving you that
flexibility is key.

00:10:59.040 --> 00:11:01.530
And that is the foundation
of any hybrid solution,

00:11:01.530 --> 00:11:05.495
that you have a strong,
scalable global network.

00:11:05.495 --> 00:11:07.620
The other thing that's
important-- and this is more

00:11:07.620 --> 00:11:10.680
of a positioning piece around
how we think about hybrid--

00:11:10.680 --> 00:11:14.412
is that fundamentally, hybrid
if it's going to be effective,

00:11:14.412 --> 00:11:16.370
it has to work in a lot
of different locations.

00:11:16.370 --> 00:11:18.780
It has to come to you and
meet you where you're at.

00:11:18.780 --> 00:11:20.940
So a principle
here-- and you saw

00:11:20.940 --> 00:11:22.830
this in the Cloud
Services Platform--

00:11:22.830 --> 00:11:25.230
is that we want to making it
better with open innovation.

00:11:25.230 --> 00:11:27.771
So we're going to innovate with
the fastest that's out there.

00:11:27.771 --> 00:11:30.070
We're going to build
the latest technologies.

00:11:30.070 --> 00:11:32.320
We're going to give you the
latest tools on the cloud.

00:11:32.320 --> 00:11:33.570
But we're also
contributing those back

00:11:33.570 --> 00:11:35.100
into the open-source community.

00:11:35.100 --> 00:11:39.440
So you can use the
latest TensorFlow APIs

00:11:39.440 --> 00:11:42.240
and capabilities on a
machine learning engine

00:11:42.240 --> 00:11:45.820
on Google Cloud, but also you
can use it in house as well.

00:11:45.820 --> 00:11:47.880
So that's the fundamental
focus that we take

00:11:47.880 --> 00:11:49.380
and why we think
hybrid with Google

00:11:49.380 --> 00:11:50.730
makes so much sense
is because we're

00:11:50.730 --> 00:11:53.200
going to give you a common
set of tools and technologies.

00:11:53.200 --> 00:11:55.980
You don't have to
have developers

00:11:55.980 --> 00:11:58.350
that understand the
Google service then

00:11:58.350 --> 00:12:01.560
have to learn another service
for your in-house platform.

00:12:01.560 --> 00:12:04.146
So you can actually
standardize across the board.

00:12:04.146 --> 00:12:05.270
And this makes it portable.

00:12:05.270 --> 00:12:06.228
It makes it extensible.

00:12:06.228 --> 00:12:08.910
It makes it available everywhere
where you need to run.

00:12:08.910 --> 00:12:10.410
And then, of course,
this innovation

00:12:10.410 --> 00:12:11.820
that we continue
to contribute is

00:12:11.820 --> 00:12:15.310
about not just delivering value
today, but also going forward.

00:12:15.310 --> 00:12:18.060
So as we continue to
evolve Kubernetes,

00:12:18.060 --> 00:12:19.920
we will do that in
GKE, but we'll also

00:12:19.920 --> 00:12:23.710
do that in the Kubernetes
open-source project as well.

00:12:23.710 --> 00:12:27.245
So you're getting those
innovations across the board.

00:12:27.245 --> 00:12:29.120
These are some of the
key things in the last,

00:12:29.120 --> 00:12:31.654
say, six plus months we've
done in the hybrid space.

00:12:31.654 --> 00:12:33.070
I'm not going to
read all of them.

00:12:33.070 --> 00:12:34.150
This is being recorded.

00:12:34.150 --> 00:12:35.650
And I'm seeing
photos being snapped.

00:12:35.650 --> 00:12:37.732
So that's awesome.

00:12:37.732 --> 00:12:40.190
A lot of things we're doing
around the infrastructure layer

00:12:40.190 --> 00:12:43.370
are this Transfer Appliance,
Velostrata, things like that.

00:12:43.370 --> 00:12:45.390
Cloud Filestore just came out.

00:12:45.390 --> 00:12:47.759
Partner Interconnect is
for tight integration

00:12:47.759 --> 00:12:50.050
with your data center, they
give you a high throughput,

00:12:50.050 --> 00:12:51.640
highly scalable
network, and then,

00:12:51.640 --> 00:12:53.920
of course, five
plus or five regions

00:12:53.920 --> 00:12:58.180
we've added onto the platform
in the last six or seven months.

00:12:58.180 --> 00:13:00.070
On the application
layer, support

00:13:00.070 --> 00:13:04.180
for things like Shared VPC, a
MemoryStore service for caching

00:13:04.180 --> 00:13:06.550
in the platform,
Pub/Sub emulators

00:13:06.550 --> 00:13:09.250
for various tools that
are common like Kafka,

00:13:09.250 --> 00:13:11.530
private clusters to support
your architecture the way

00:13:11.530 --> 00:13:14.415
it fits from an
application perspective.

00:13:14.415 --> 00:13:15.790
And then in the
management space,

00:13:15.790 --> 00:13:18.370
I think you saw a lot in
the Cloud Services Platform.

00:13:18.370 --> 00:13:22.030
But even before that, we've
already been working strongly

00:13:22.030 --> 00:13:25.120
on integrating Istio with
Apigee, having Apigee

00:13:25.120 --> 00:13:26.330
expand to multiple regions.

00:13:26.330 --> 00:13:29.680
We have a way to expose that
API layer over your applications

00:13:29.680 --> 00:13:31.090
around the globe.

00:13:31.090 --> 00:13:33.576
And then all the work in
IM in terms of conditions,

00:13:33.576 --> 00:13:35.200
resource management
so you can not only

00:13:35.200 --> 00:13:37.670
deploy your infrastructure
where you need it,

00:13:37.670 --> 00:13:40.900
but also manage it in alignment
with your own security

00:13:40.900 --> 00:13:43.750
practices and principles that
you have inside your enterprise

00:13:43.750 --> 00:13:45.837
today.

00:13:45.837 --> 00:13:47.670
And, of course, the
Cloud Services Platform,

00:13:47.670 --> 00:13:51.007
which you've seen-- who's
seen that in the keynote?

00:13:51.007 --> 00:13:53.590
All right, you're obviously all
asleep because I saw no hands.

00:13:53.590 --> 00:13:54.440
Somebody saw it.

00:13:54.440 --> 00:13:55.530
He's saw it.

00:13:55.530 --> 00:13:57.240
That's two, four.

00:13:57.240 --> 00:13:59.610
We're getting there, six.

00:13:59.610 --> 00:14:02.040
So the idea here
is we want to take

00:14:02.040 --> 00:14:05.957
what we've done in the
open-source projects like Istio

00:14:05.957 --> 00:14:08.040
and Kubernetes, and we
want to make that available

00:14:08.040 --> 00:14:12.210
where it's needed, which is
not only in the GCP platform,

00:14:12.210 --> 00:14:15.320
but also in your
data center today.

00:14:15.320 --> 00:14:17.767
And so, the Cloud Build
function, the Cloud Build

00:14:17.767 --> 00:14:19.350
and serverless
capabilities for people

00:14:19.350 --> 00:14:22.140
who are builders that want
an integrated workflow,

00:14:22.140 --> 00:14:25.800
serverless as baked on
top of the GKE platform,

00:14:25.800 --> 00:14:29.100
GKE On-Prem which gives you
the ability to run Kubernetes

00:14:29.100 --> 00:14:33.570
both in the cloud and on-prem
in a consistent way, Stackdriver

00:14:33.570 --> 00:14:35.610
and Istio integration,
so you actually

00:14:35.610 --> 00:14:40.050
have the ability to see API
usage across your existing

00:14:40.050 --> 00:14:43.350
infrastructure internally,
but also in the cloud.

00:14:43.350 --> 00:14:45.709
And then fully managed Istio
is another key piece there.

00:14:45.709 --> 00:14:48.000
And of course, this all
integrating with the Apigee API

00:14:48.000 --> 00:14:49.920
management gives you a
single management plan

00:14:49.920 --> 00:14:52.130
across multiple environments.

00:14:52.130 --> 00:14:54.540
And so the way I look
at this is I actually

00:14:54.540 --> 00:14:56.065
drew an architecture diagram.

00:14:56.065 --> 00:14:56.640
Why not?

00:14:56.640 --> 00:14:58.816
And so you see here,
there's a whole bunch

00:14:58.816 --> 00:14:59.940
of approaches to the cloud.

00:14:59.940 --> 00:15:02.523
And they're all represented now
as part of this Cloud Services

00:15:02.523 --> 00:15:04.050
Platform architecture.

00:15:04.050 --> 00:15:06.270
So here you've got
your Legacy/Cloud-Nope,

00:15:06.270 --> 00:15:07.690
the things that never move.

00:15:07.690 --> 00:15:09.282
They're very
exciting over there.

00:15:09.282 --> 00:15:10.240
We'll leave them alone.

00:15:10.240 --> 00:15:12.240
But they can actually
still connect to the cloud

00:15:12.240 --> 00:15:14.700
with a Dedicated Interconnect
and then using the Apigee API

00:15:14.700 --> 00:15:17.940
platform as a way to expose
legacy services with a web

00:15:17.940 --> 00:15:20.970
service interface, a
very common approach

00:15:20.970 --> 00:15:23.222
to help people expose
something that's maybe--

00:15:23.222 --> 00:15:24.930
they want a way to
call into it, but they

00:15:24.930 --> 00:15:26.370
don't want to have
to rewrite it,

00:15:26.370 --> 00:15:28.866
so put an API layer
over the front.

00:15:28.866 --> 00:15:32.600
And then staying on-prem in
the enterprise data center,

00:15:32.600 --> 00:15:35.646
you can use GKE On-Prem
to modernize in place.

00:15:35.646 --> 00:15:37.020
I want to improve
my application,

00:15:37.020 --> 00:15:38.735
move to a modernized
architecture,

00:15:38.735 --> 00:15:40.860
but I don't want to move
it out of the data center.

00:15:40.860 --> 00:15:43.327
Maybe it's tightly
coupled to other services

00:15:43.327 --> 00:15:44.160
in the architecture.

00:15:44.160 --> 00:15:45.450
I can leave that there.

00:15:45.450 --> 00:15:49.510
But I can add a managed Istio
mesh over the top of it.

00:15:49.510 --> 00:15:51.600
So I can actually
control API access,

00:15:51.600 --> 00:15:54.176
even though it's
in my data center.

00:15:54.176 --> 00:15:55.800
And then, of course,
that same approach

00:15:55.800 --> 00:15:57.370
can be applied to
my remote site.

00:15:57.370 --> 00:16:01.740
So for example, I might
be a retail organization.

00:16:01.740 --> 00:16:03.672
And I have hundreds of stores.

00:16:03.672 --> 00:16:04.380
Well, guess what?

00:16:04.380 --> 00:16:06.660
I got to still control
the services running

00:16:06.660 --> 00:16:08.877
in the GK On-Prem
in those stores

00:16:08.877 --> 00:16:11.460
as well just like I control the
services in my enterprise data

00:16:11.460 --> 00:16:12.540
center.

00:16:12.540 --> 00:16:14.040
So you have that
common model there.

00:16:14.040 --> 00:16:16.600
And then here in
the Cloud Platform,

00:16:16.600 --> 00:16:18.400
you might do
lift-and-shift where

00:16:18.400 --> 00:16:21.620
you bring some things over with
Velostrata, but also the move

00:16:21.620 --> 00:16:24.120
and improve, like I want to
move some features over and then

00:16:24.120 --> 00:16:26.400
improve how they run
today, tying that

00:16:26.400 --> 00:16:27.950
into the managed Istio.

00:16:27.950 --> 00:16:30.150
And what you get with
this particular approach

00:16:30.150 --> 00:16:32.340
from a hybrid
perspective is, one,

00:16:32.340 --> 00:16:33.870
you get central
cluster management.

00:16:33.870 --> 00:16:36.780
Now you can control
the Kubernetes that's

00:16:36.780 --> 00:16:38.560
running across all
of your environments,

00:16:38.560 --> 00:16:40.170
not just the one
that's in the cloud.

00:16:40.170 --> 00:16:41.628
You have a centralized
way in which

00:16:41.628 --> 00:16:44.140
you can view how those
clusters are performing,

00:16:44.140 --> 00:16:48.540
what they're doing, and
upgrade them across the board.

00:16:48.540 --> 00:16:51.930
You also have a Cloud Build
with a common tool chain.

00:16:51.930 --> 00:16:54.720
So as I build containers,
as I build those artifacts,

00:16:54.720 --> 00:16:56.803
and I want to deploy them,
I now have a common set

00:16:56.803 --> 00:16:59.680
of services and APIs that
I can use across the board.

00:16:59.680 --> 00:17:03.159
And I can deploy that to
any of those clusters.

00:17:03.159 --> 00:17:04.950
And then the other
thing that's fundamental

00:17:04.950 --> 00:17:07.640
to a true hybrid model is a
centralized API management,

00:17:07.640 --> 00:17:13.020
this ability to capture who can
access what in my architecture.

00:17:13.020 --> 00:17:15.180
So if you go down the
microservices model,

00:17:15.180 --> 00:17:17.819
the ability to
say front end here

00:17:17.819 --> 00:17:22.109
can call back end there, and
control throughput rates,

00:17:22.109 --> 00:17:25.859
access to those various services
all through a centralized API

00:17:25.859 --> 00:17:28.061
management [INAUDIBLE].

00:17:28.061 --> 00:17:30.060
Now, that's obviously a
little forward thinking.

00:17:30.060 --> 00:17:31.685
That was our announcement
this morning.

00:17:31.685 --> 00:17:33.755
That's where I see hybrid
going to some degree.

00:17:33.755 --> 00:17:35.880
And there's also a lot of
other tools in the space.

00:17:35.880 --> 00:17:37.171
And we'll hear more about that.

00:17:37.171 --> 00:17:39.840
But what we're seeing in
terms of real use cases today,

00:17:39.840 --> 00:17:41.660
I thought I'd cover
that just so folks can

00:17:41.660 --> 00:17:43.470
get excited about
what might apply

00:17:43.470 --> 00:17:45.412
to their particular
business today.

00:17:45.412 --> 00:17:47.970
So there's a few that
we see quite commonly.

00:17:47.970 --> 00:17:50.730
One is this idea of invoking
legacy dependencies, moving

00:17:50.730 --> 00:17:52.990
pieces of my architecture
into the cloud,

00:17:52.990 --> 00:17:55.200
but calling back into
existing services

00:17:55.200 --> 00:17:57.430
that I can't move today
or don't want to--

00:17:57.430 --> 00:18:00.690
mainframes, large-scale
packaged applications

00:18:00.690 --> 00:18:03.280
that have too many dependencies
to be picked up today,

00:18:03.280 --> 00:18:05.340
things that maybe
run on infrastructure

00:18:05.340 --> 00:18:07.380
that is not cloud ready.

00:18:07.380 --> 00:18:09.540
But I want to still tap
into that from the cloud.

00:18:09.540 --> 00:18:13.110
That's a pretty common front
end calling into a back end

00:18:13.110 --> 00:18:14.310
model in hybrid.

00:18:14.310 --> 00:18:16.500
Another is
cross-environment execution.

00:18:16.500 --> 00:18:18.690
So I want all the
benefits of CI/CD.

00:18:18.690 --> 00:18:20.870
And I want it fantastic
dev test process.

00:18:20.870 --> 00:18:22.920
I want flexibility for
my development teams.

00:18:22.920 --> 00:18:24.090
I want to move quickly.

00:18:24.090 --> 00:18:25.756
But it turns out, I
still need to deploy

00:18:25.756 --> 00:18:27.690
that code in my oil rig.

00:18:27.690 --> 00:18:29.370
Well, that's going
to be a model.

00:18:29.370 --> 00:18:31.911
You're not going to change your
oil rig necessarily tomorrow,

00:18:31.911 --> 00:18:35.840
so, hey, the ability to use the
cloud as a cross-environment,

00:18:35.840 --> 00:18:37.960
execution development
environment

00:18:37.960 --> 00:18:39.540
is a common platform.

00:18:39.540 --> 00:18:42.480
But deploying directly
from there to the edge

00:18:42.480 --> 00:18:44.152
is common as well.

00:18:44.152 --> 00:18:45.610
And then edge
computing in general,

00:18:45.610 --> 00:18:47.220
I want to add a whole
bunch of sensors,

00:18:47.220 --> 00:18:48.510
capabilities to my architecture.

00:18:48.510 --> 00:18:50.270
I want to gather a
whole bunch of new data

00:18:50.270 --> 00:18:51.720
that I've never
processed before.

00:18:51.720 --> 00:18:54.210
I probably don't have the
processing power or the storage

00:18:54.210 --> 00:18:55.040
that I need.

00:18:55.040 --> 00:18:57.840
How can I use the cloud as a
way to extend my architecture

00:18:57.840 --> 00:19:00.840
to capture all that information,
aggregate it, feed it back

00:19:00.840 --> 00:19:02.670
into my existing
application, and then use

00:19:02.670 --> 00:19:05.940
that as a blended hybrid model?

00:19:05.940 --> 00:19:09.430
So some examples that, you
see that GCP front end.

00:19:09.430 --> 00:19:12.240
I've got my back end
services, my existing stuff.

00:19:12.240 --> 00:19:14.200
I then have all my
client traffic coming

00:19:14.200 --> 00:19:15.640
through global load balancers.

00:19:15.640 --> 00:19:18.310
I can hit whatever region
is closest to my customers.

00:19:18.310 --> 00:19:21.447
I can capture all the front end
work that I need to do on that.

00:19:21.447 --> 00:19:23.530
And then when I need to,
I can use an interconnect

00:19:23.530 --> 00:19:26.470
back or a VPN back into
my existing architecture

00:19:26.470 --> 00:19:29.440
and tap into other
systems like, say, ERP,

00:19:29.440 --> 00:19:32.625
or back end inventory
management, things like that.

00:19:32.625 --> 00:19:34.750
That's a pretty common
scenario we see quite a bit.

00:19:34.750 --> 00:19:37.420
It might apply to what
you're doing today.

00:19:37.420 --> 00:19:40.169
The other one we see,
as I mentioned, IoT.

00:19:40.169 --> 00:19:41.710
Sure my devices are
going to continue

00:19:41.710 --> 00:19:46.660
to reach back into my existing
on-prem infrastructure.

00:19:46.660 --> 00:19:49.030
But then at the same time,
I can use Cloud Pub/Sub,

00:19:49.030 --> 00:19:50.930
IoT Core, Dataflow.

00:19:50.930 --> 00:19:54.610
And there's a whole array of
analytics and storage services

00:19:54.610 --> 00:19:58.480
to capture, process, and then
aggregate that data so that I

00:19:58.480 --> 00:20:01.870
can feed the results
back into my existing

00:20:01.870 --> 00:20:05.600
architecture for maybe
output back to my devices.

00:20:05.600 --> 00:20:08.680
So I have this ability
to take this maybe

00:20:08.680 --> 00:20:11.770
smaller-scale footprint and then
add a whole bunch of managed

00:20:11.770 --> 00:20:15.610
services and data processing
capability as a hybrid model

00:20:15.610 --> 00:20:18.050
building, obviously, a hybrid
network interconnecting

00:20:18.050 --> 00:20:21.184
between those two.

00:20:21.184 --> 00:20:22.850
Some other examples
we're seeing is just

00:20:22.850 --> 00:20:25.400
tapping into the
global network of GCP

00:20:25.400 --> 00:20:27.872
as a way to get footprint
around the globe.

00:20:27.872 --> 00:20:29.330
As you expand your
business, as you

00:20:29.330 --> 00:20:31.970
expand to where your
customers are coming from,

00:20:31.970 --> 00:20:34.125
you want this
availability everywhere.

00:20:34.125 --> 00:20:35.750
You want to be able
to have the closest

00:20:35.750 --> 00:20:36.890
location be tapped into.

00:20:36.890 --> 00:20:39.440
So this global availability
of the 17 regions

00:20:39.440 --> 00:20:40.490
is a huge benefit.

00:20:40.490 --> 00:20:42.350
You can go from
tapping into one region

00:20:42.350 --> 00:20:44.480
to get into that
network we saw earlier

00:20:44.480 --> 00:20:46.070
to get around the globe.

00:20:46.070 --> 00:20:48.030
Another is jurisdictional
or data sovereignty.

00:20:48.030 --> 00:20:49.700
You might be tapping
into a new market.

00:20:49.700 --> 00:20:52.116
And there might be rules about
where the data has to live,

00:20:52.116 --> 00:20:55.160
has to be processed, and having
regions there available to you

00:20:55.160 --> 00:20:56.420
right away, great example.

00:20:56.420 --> 00:20:58.820
But you still want
visibility coming back

00:20:58.820 --> 00:21:02.240
into your central
IT infrastructure,

00:21:02.240 --> 00:21:06.290
so blending those data storage
and processing requirements

00:21:06.290 --> 00:21:10.502
with tying it back into your
existing centralized approach

00:21:10.502 --> 00:21:11.210
and architecture.

00:21:11.210 --> 00:21:12.918
And then the last,
which is quite common,

00:21:12.918 --> 00:21:14.810
is this idea of
extending an architecture

00:21:14.810 --> 00:21:16.580
with cloud services.

00:21:16.580 --> 00:21:18.260
I think IoT is one example.

00:21:18.260 --> 00:21:20.990
I'll dive in another
one here, which is--

00:21:20.990 --> 00:21:23.255
actually, let me
go back real fast.

00:21:23.255 --> 00:21:25.880
Another one here, which is this
idea of, let's say I want to ML

00:21:25.880 --> 00:21:28.250
or I want to add big
data, these things

00:21:28.250 --> 00:21:30.550
are things I can tap
into my architecture.

00:21:30.550 --> 00:21:31.850
And I don't have to rebuild it.

00:21:31.850 --> 00:21:33.475
I just have to figure
out a way to pump

00:21:33.475 --> 00:21:37.320
that data across and bring the
results back into my platform.

00:21:37.320 --> 00:21:39.550
So in terms of
regional availability,

00:21:39.550 --> 00:21:42.020
you can imagine
this kind of a model

00:21:42.020 --> 00:21:44.000
where you have a
global load balancer,

00:21:44.000 --> 00:21:46.730
you're exposing to
customers across the globe,

00:21:46.730 --> 00:21:49.520
and then you're building
proxies in the regions, running

00:21:49.520 --> 00:21:54.784
individual localized services
for various performance needs,

00:21:54.784 --> 00:21:56.450
but then fundamentally
tapping that back

00:21:56.450 --> 00:21:58.850
into an interconnect in terms
of feeding that data back

00:21:58.850 --> 00:22:04.760
into your maybe centralized
or a lower-lessened footprint

00:22:04.760 --> 00:22:06.920
enterprise platform.

00:22:06.920 --> 00:22:09.660
The other one being the big
data example we talked about.

00:22:09.660 --> 00:22:11.225
Yeah, I've got a
ton of applications

00:22:11.225 --> 00:22:13.310
out over here that
don't fit on the screen.

00:22:13.310 --> 00:22:15.518
I didn't think the screen
was going to be big enough.

00:22:15.518 --> 00:22:17.400
So I was wrong about
that obviously.

00:22:17.400 --> 00:22:19.760
So [INAUDIBLE] those
apps over here.

00:22:19.760 --> 00:22:21.500
And then that data
is being pumped

00:22:21.500 --> 00:22:23.580
through over a variety of
different capabilities,

00:22:23.580 --> 00:22:25.520
whether it be a transfer
appliance, a transfer

00:22:25.520 --> 00:22:28.460
service, or just a VPN
interconnect, whatever

00:22:28.460 --> 00:22:32.630
makes sense into an event
stream, ETL streaming, staging

00:22:32.630 --> 00:22:36.200
buckets, various streaming and
storage services that give you

00:22:36.200 --> 00:22:39.340
your time series analysis, your
data warehouse on BigQuery.

00:22:39.340 --> 00:22:41.649
And then you can build a
whole analytics and reporting

00:22:41.649 --> 00:22:43.940
dashboard over top of that,
which can be exposed either

00:22:43.940 --> 00:22:44.450
in the cloud.

00:22:44.450 --> 00:22:46.033
Or you can even pump
some of that data

00:22:46.033 --> 00:22:47.810
back for reporting on-prem.

00:22:47.810 --> 00:22:50.510
So another common,
classic hybrid model we're

00:22:50.510 --> 00:22:53.430
seeing in the market today.

00:22:53.430 --> 00:22:55.680
So this is just a summary
slide that shows them again.

00:22:55.680 --> 00:22:57.410
There you go.

00:22:57.410 --> 00:22:58.940
But fundamentally,
we're obviously

00:22:58.940 --> 00:23:02.029
doing a lot for the
Cloud Services Platform.

00:23:02.029 --> 00:23:04.070
And we've been doing a
lot in terms of commitment

00:23:04.070 --> 00:23:07.190
to open-source tools
and technologies.

00:23:07.190 --> 00:23:10.010
But we also want the platform
to be open for our partners

00:23:10.010 --> 00:23:11.390
as well.

00:23:11.390 --> 00:23:13.370
Hybrid fundamentally
means a blending

00:23:13.370 --> 00:23:14.960
of disparate technologies.

00:23:14.960 --> 00:23:16.460
And we recognize
that many of you

00:23:16.460 --> 00:23:18.830
are working with
existing partners today.

00:23:18.830 --> 00:23:22.610
You have a wide array of
services or capabilities

00:23:22.610 --> 00:23:26.760
that you're trying to bridge
into the Cloud platform.

00:23:26.760 --> 00:23:29.450
And so we think that
it's as fundamental

00:23:29.450 --> 00:23:31.820
that we actually make this
platform extensible and allow

00:23:31.820 --> 00:23:35.000
others to participate as well
so your best choice tools

00:23:35.000 --> 00:23:37.020
are available for you as well.

00:23:37.020 --> 00:23:40.040
So whether it be multicloud
management capabilities--

00:23:40.040 --> 00:23:43.700
that's why we expose
all of our APIs,

00:23:43.700 --> 00:23:46.850
so you can actually tap into
that from a variety of tools

00:23:46.850 --> 00:23:48.650
and management consoles.

00:23:48.650 --> 00:23:50.210
Hybrid applications,
when you want

00:23:50.210 --> 00:23:53.780
to run containers everywhere,
GKE allows you to do that.

00:23:53.780 --> 00:23:56.280
You can run Kubernetes on-prem.

00:23:56.280 --> 00:23:59.630
You can run it with
other hosting partners

00:23:59.630 --> 00:24:02.600
as well, hybrid services
across the board in terms

00:24:02.600 --> 00:24:05.690
of securing and monitoring
services across clouds,

00:24:05.690 --> 00:24:08.730
and then hybrid infrastructure,
so that commitment to VMs

00:24:08.730 --> 00:24:13.580
that can move around
V2V, V2C, that Cloud

00:24:13.580 --> 00:24:15.710
Interconnect and data
migration are fundamentals.

00:24:15.710 --> 00:24:17.210
And obviously, we
have a whole bunch

00:24:17.210 --> 00:24:18.840
of partners in this space.

00:24:18.840 --> 00:24:21.980
But what I'd love to do is turn
it over to one of our partners

00:24:21.980 --> 00:24:27.664
right now to talk more about
his story and their solutions.

00:24:27.664 --> 00:24:29.330
Sridhar Devarapalli
is a general manager

00:24:29.330 --> 00:24:30.734
of cloud services in Nutanix.

00:24:30.734 --> 00:24:31.400
Thanks, Sridhar.

