00:25:01.310 --> 00:25:02.250
Can I see?

00:25:02.250 --> 00:25:03.260
OK, that's quite a few.

00:25:03.260 --> 00:25:04.340
That's quite a few.

00:25:04.340 --> 00:25:07.040
So let's start
there, just to recap

00:25:07.040 --> 00:25:09.700
what Nutanix does as
a company and what's

00:25:09.700 --> 00:25:12.890
our mission statement.

00:25:12.890 --> 00:25:15.710
We deliver infrastructure,
data center infrastructure

00:25:15.710 --> 00:25:17.960
for a customer's
on-premises environment.

00:25:17.960 --> 00:25:22.390
And our mission is really to
bring cloud-like simplicity,

00:25:22.390 --> 00:25:25.940
and agility, and cost economics
to on-premises data center

00:25:25.940 --> 00:25:26.950
environment.

00:25:26.950 --> 00:25:29.380
And we build a
turnkey infrastructure

00:25:29.380 --> 00:25:31.750
stack that allows
customers to focus

00:25:31.750 --> 00:25:34.360
on their applications instead
of low-level infrastructure

00:25:34.360 --> 00:25:36.100
constructs.

00:25:36.100 --> 00:25:38.410
We're about nine years old.

00:25:38.410 --> 00:25:41.037
And we've got 10,000
customers so far.

00:25:41.037 --> 00:25:42.370
And it's a fast-growing company.

00:25:45.130 --> 00:25:47.740
So how do we
accomplish our mission?

00:25:47.740 --> 00:25:51.190
What do we do to
realize our goals?

00:25:51.190 --> 00:25:53.380
Well, to begin with,
we took a deep look

00:25:53.380 --> 00:25:56.260
at data center technology
choices built and delivered

00:25:56.260 --> 00:25:59.980
piecemeal to customers
and the operational silos

00:25:59.980 --> 00:26:00.970
that they create.

00:26:00.970 --> 00:26:02.470
Traditional
architecture is required

00:26:02.470 --> 00:26:05.210
to plan and procure,
compute, storage, networking,

00:26:05.210 --> 00:26:07.984
virtualization, all
of these independently

00:26:07.984 --> 00:26:10.150
and manage the lifecycle
of each of these components

00:26:10.150 --> 00:26:11.170
independently.

00:26:11.170 --> 00:26:13.870
That results in long
lead times, dedicated

00:26:13.870 --> 00:26:16.750
teams, specialized
skills, and, in general,

00:26:16.750 --> 00:26:20.440
a pretty big operational hazard.

00:26:20.440 --> 00:26:21.370
So what have we done?

00:26:21.370 --> 00:26:23.380
We went ahead and
designed a new system

00:26:23.380 --> 00:26:25.090
that coalesced all
those technologies

00:26:25.090 --> 00:26:26.790
that you see on the left--

00:26:26.790 --> 00:26:29.830
compute, storage, networking,
virtualization, management,

00:26:29.830 --> 00:26:31.810
automation, orchestration--

00:26:31.810 --> 00:26:35.980
and built an integrated, single,
turnkey appliance form factor.

00:26:35.980 --> 00:26:38.560
And the result of that is
its unparalleled simplicity

00:26:38.560 --> 00:26:41.829
as far as data center operations
are concerned and planning

00:26:41.829 --> 00:26:42.370
is concerned.

00:26:42.370 --> 00:26:45.210
And the value for customers
is evident from the rate

00:26:45.210 --> 00:26:48.550
at which the company's
been growing with time.

00:26:48.550 --> 00:26:52.510
Having said that, now what are
we doing with Google and GCP?

00:26:52.510 --> 00:26:56.990
What's our joint vision?

00:26:56.990 --> 00:26:59.190
I can get to that as--
yeah, there you go.

00:26:59.190 --> 00:27:02.400
Our goal is to deliver a
seamless one-click hybrid cloud

00:27:02.400 --> 00:27:03.960
experience and
platform for customers

00:27:03.960 --> 00:27:06.120
to right-size their
applications and workloads

00:27:06.120 --> 00:27:07.980
between private
and public clouds.

00:27:07.980 --> 00:27:09.360
What exactly do we mean by that?

00:27:09.360 --> 00:27:09.960
Essentially.

00:27:09.960 --> 00:27:12.360
As you start from your
on-premises data center

00:27:12.360 --> 00:27:15.420
environments, and you try
to figure out an access path

00:27:15.420 --> 00:27:18.090
to the public cloud to realize
very specific hybrid use

00:27:18.090 --> 00:27:20.530
cases-- and we'll talk more
about what those use cases

00:27:20.530 --> 00:27:21.420
are--

00:27:21.420 --> 00:27:23.550
we want to give you the
best seamless experience

00:27:23.550 --> 00:27:26.130
possible without having to
go through the complexities

00:27:26.130 --> 00:27:28.800
and transformations,
and redesigning,

00:27:28.800 --> 00:27:30.300
and refactoring applications.

00:27:30.300 --> 00:27:33.750
So that's the access path
that we've conceived.

00:27:33.750 --> 00:27:36.030
And we'll talk more about
how exactly are we going

00:27:36.030 --> 00:27:38.550
about realizing this vision.

00:27:38.550 --> 00:27:40.440
There are a set of
capabilities that we're

00:27:40.440 --> 00:27:42.160
offering to make
this hybrid journey

00:27:42.160 --> 00:27:43.420
seamless for our customers.

00:27:43.420 --> 00:27:46.870
And I'll break it down into
these three broad areas.

00:27:46.870 --> 00:27:49.812
One is you've got to start
with transforming your own data

00:27:49.812 --> 00:27:52.020
center because that's where
most of your applications

00:27:52.020 --> 00:27:53.130
are running today.

00:27:53.130 --> 00:27:56.580
And what have you done to
bring a degree of simplicity

00:27:56.580 --> 00:27:58.090
and agility to that environment?

00:27:58.090 --> 00:28:00.000
And that's where
Nutanix as a company

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:02.000
is bringing value
to our customers.

00:28:02.000 --> 00:28:04.620
We're proven to provide
agility and simplicity

00:28:04.620 --> 00:28:07.720
and transform your existing data
center to be more cloud-like.

00:28:07.720 --> 00:28:09.480
That's been our
mission all along

00:28:09.480 --> 00:28:11.520
and the success
path for us anyway.

00:28:11.520 --> 00:28:14.410
Now, in addition to
transforming data centers,

00:28:14.410 --> 00:28:16.560
we are also allowing
customers to transform

00:28:16.560 --> 00:28:18.570
their applications,
redesigning them

00:28:18.570 --> 00:28:21.360
to be more microservices-like
using technologies

00:28:21.360 --> 00:28:24.900
like Kubernetes delivered
natively on-premises off

00:28:24.900 --> 00:28:27.120
of our appliances.

00:28:27.120 --> 00:28:30.600
Think of that as Google
services delivered natively out

00:28:30.600 --> 00:28:32.280
of Nutanix platforms.

00:28:32.280 --> 00:28:34.190
And our goal
eventually is that you

00:28:34.190 --> 00:28:37.530
consume a combination of
Nutanix and Google services

00:28:37.530 --> 00:28:38.970
irrespective of the location.

00:28:38.970 --> 00:28:41.070
They could be
on-premises, off-premises.

00:28:41.070 --> 00:28:44.850
But in the end, you can start
building your applications

00:28:44.850 --> 00:28:48.600
in a way that you
progressively modernize them

00:28:48.600 --> 00:28:53.035
without having to worry about
location and transformation.

00:28:53.035 --> 00:28:55.410
Another key aspect, which is
related to what I just said,

00:28:55.410 --> 00:28:57.900
is the ability to move
applications back and forth

00:28:57.900 --> 00:28:59.310
between on-premises
and the cloud

00:28:59.310 --> 00:29:03.687
without having to, again,
redesign or retransform them.

00:29:03.687 --> 00:29:06.270
There's an offering that's new
that we call Xi Cloud Services.

00:29:06.270 --> 00:29:08.790
And I'll talk more
about what that means.

00:29:08.790 --> 00:29:12.930
Nutanix Stack for GCP,
Nutanix Xi for GCP integrated

00:29:12.930 --> 00:29:15.900
with our on-premises offering
allow you to move workloads

00:29:15.900 --> 00:29:19.686
back and forth as
your business requires

00:29:19.686 --> 00:29:21.060
without having to
go through many

00:29:21.060 --> 00:29:23.640
of the typical complexities
that one goes through

00:29:23.640 --> 00:29:25.570
for cloud migration.

00:29:25.570 --> 00:29:28.230
And related to
that is essentially

00:29:28.230 --> 00:29:31.590
orchestration and automation of
placement of these workloads.

00:29:31.590 --> 00:29:34.380
And I'll talk more about how
another product of Nutanix,

00:29:34.380 --> 00:29:39.090
which is Calm, plays a big
part in solving that problem.

00:29:39.090 --> 00:29:42.295
And finally, eventually
you want to get to a place

00:29:42.295 --> 00:29:43.920
where you've refactored
the application

00:29:43.920 --> 00:29:45.474
to optimally run in the cloud.

00:29:45.474 --> 00:29:46.890
Therefore, you're
taking advantage

00:29:46.890 --> 00:29:49.770
of all the native
cloud services and not

00:29:49.770 --> 00:29:52.320
just doing the lift-and-shift
from on-premises to the cloud.

00:29:52.320 --> 00:29:55.560
And we help you progressively
refactor those applications

00:29:55.560 --> 00:29:59.730
through a series of
tightly integrated services

00:29:59.730 --> 00:30:03.300
between Google and Nutanix.

00:30:03.300 --> 00:30:06.850
So at the high level,
those are the capabilities

00:30:06.850 --> 00:30:10.000
that we're designing together.

00:30:10.000 --> 00:30:14.250
Now, what use cases do
they enable you to solve?

00:30:14.250 --> 00:30:16.260
Again as I said,
any use case that

00:30:16.260 --> 00:30:18.900
starts with your
on-premises environment that

00:30:18.900 --> 00:30:21.660
needs to access the cloud
as a seamless extension

00:30:21.660 --> 00:30:23.730
of your on-premises
environment would benefit

00:30:23.730 --> 00:30:26.060
from these joint offerings.

00:30:26.060 --> 00:30:28.680
The first and foremost,
disaster recovery and business

00:30:28.680 --> 00:30:29.580
continuity.

00:30:29.580 --> 00:30:33.840
This has been a problem
that's pretty hard

00:30:33.840 --> 00:30:38.170
to address traditionally
because it's complex in nature.

00:30:38.170 --> 00:30:41.544
And what we've done is we've
built a DR to the cloud

00:30:41.544 --> 00:30:42.960
solution that's
tightly integrated

00:30:42.960 --> 00:30:44.210
with our on-premises offering.

00:30:44.210 --> 00:30:46.740
And with Google and
Nutanix together, you

00:30:46.740 --> 00:30:52.410
can leverage the GCP
cloud as a DR target

00:30:52.410 --> 00:30:54.340
in the case of a failure.

00:30:54.340 --> 00:30:57.540
A related use case to
DR is lift-and-shift.

00:30:57.540 --> 00:30:59.410
As we talk to many
customers, they're

00:30:59.410 --> 00:31:01.520
looking to miniaturize
their data center footprint

00:31:01.520 --> 00:31:04.500
and trying to figure out
a way to move workloads

00:31:04.500 --> 00:31:07.380
into the cloud while retaining
some existing workloads

00:31:07.380 --> 00:31:08.280
on-premises.

00:31:08.280 --> 00:31:10.320
How do we make that
motion seamless

00:31:10.320 --> 00:31:12.360
and allow you to
still have backward

00:31:12.360 --> 00:31:15.180
connectivity and accessibility
to what's running on-prem

00:31:15.180 --> 00:31:19.380
so that in the end you realize
that true hybrid situation

00:31:19.380 --> 00:31:22.860
where your actual
workloads are spanning both

00:31:22.860 --> 00:31:25.170
the public and private
cloud without you having

00:31:25.170 --> 00:31:27.900
to go through, again,
a series of investments

00:31:27.900 --> 00:31:30.500
and get us into migration
technologies and so forth?

00:31:33.280 --> 00:31:34.960
And another use case
is cloud bursting.

00:31:34.960 --> 00:31:37.740
And we'll talk about
a specific example

00:31:37.740 --> 00:31:41.190
where there's a customer
who actually wanted

00:31:41.190 --> 00:31:44.610
additional capacity in the cloud
based on fluctuating demand

00:31:44.610 --> 00:31:46.650
from their users.

00:31:46.650 --> 00:31:48.340
And how do we enable
that use case?

00:31:48.340 --> 00:31:51.660
By allowing you to expand
into the cloud just as much

00:31:51.660 --> 00:31:54.670
as your applications
need as though that's

00:31:54.670 --> 00:31:57.852
a seamless extension of
your existing platforms.

00:31:57.852 --> 00:31:59.310
So these are some
of the use cases.

00:31:59.310 --> 00:32:02.430
And the broad theme is
that any use case that

00:32:02.430 --> 00:32:05.490
is gravitated in your
on-premises environment

00:32:05.490 --> 00:32:08.700
but needs either intermittent
or event-driven access

00:32:08.700 --> 00:32:12.000
to public cloud services
will really benefit

00:32:12.000 --> 00:32:13.538
from these joint offerings.

00:32:20.370 --> 00:32:22.250
I got stuck a little bit here.

00:32:41.300 --> 00:32:42.556
All right, sorry about that.

00:32:42.556 --> 00:32:44.430
There was a little bit
of a technical glitch.

00:32:44.430 --> 00:32:47.600
So, I spoke about
the why and the what.

00:32:47.600 --> 00:32:50.120
Why are we embarking
on this partnership?

00:32:50.120 --> 00:32:52.190
And what use cases
can be addressed?

00:32:52.190 --> 00:32:53.930
Let's talk a little
bit about the how.

00:32:53.930 --> 00:32:56.420
What product offering
specifically will

00:32:56.420 --> 00:32:59.260
help us address the use cases
that I just spoke about?

00:32:59.260 --> 00:33:01.610
And the centerpiece
of it all is actually

00:33:01.610 --> 00:33:03.740
to enable a seamless
hybrid experience.

00:33:03.740 --> 00:33:05.660
The first thing to
solve for is to keep

00:33:05.660 --> 00:33:08.450
the same operational
paradigm, the same interfaces,

00:33:08.450 --> 00:33:10.625
the same tooling,
the same workflows

00:33:10.625 --> 00:33:13.729
to manage your applications
independent of location.

00:33:13.729 --> 00:33:15.520
And there's a product
offering from Nutanix

00:33:15.520 --> 00:33:18.740
called Calm, which stands for
Cloud Application Lifecycle

00:33:18.740 --> 00:33:19.727
Management.

00:33:19.727 --> 00:33:22.310
And it allows you to manage the
lifecycle of your applications

00:33:22.310 --> 00:33:23.750
across multiple clouds.

00:33:23.750 --> 00:33:26.780
And by lifecycle I mean
application deployment,

00:33:26.780 --> 00:33:29.846
upgrades, expansion,
visibility, security.

00:33:29.846 --> 00:33:31.220
All those elements
that you would

00:33:31.220 --> 00:33:33.890
take care of as you design
and run your applications,

00:33:33.890 --> 00:33:36.860
Calm gives you a
consistent interface

00:33:36.860 --> 00:33:40.040
to perform all those operations
across different clouds.

00:33:40.040 --> 00:33:41.540
And the way you
would do that is you

00:33:41.540 --> 00:33:43.610
would create a Calm blueprint.

00:33:43.610 --> 00:33:46.190
Think of that as a
set of instructions

00:33:46.190 --> 00:33:48.860
that describes the constitution
of your application

00:33:48.860 --> 00:33:51.200
and its infrastructure
requirements and dependencies.

00:33:51.200 --> 00:33:53.360
And once you've designed
and built the blueprint,

00:33:53.360 --> 00:33:56.750
you can repeatedly deploy
it across different clouds

00:33:56.750 --> 00:33:59.900
while maintaining the same
operational consistency

00:33:59.900 --> 00:34:02.690
in the way you manage
your applications.

00:34:02.690 --> 00:34:04.870
The target cloud could be
a private cloud on Nutanix

00:34:04.870 --> 00:34:07.490
or a public cloud like GCP.

00:34:07.490 --> 00:34:09.500
And Calm allows
you to manage not

00:34:09.500 --> 00:34:11.360
just the VM-based
applications, but also

00:34:11.360 --> 00:34:13.480
containerized workloads as well.

00:34:13.480 --> 00:34:16.010
Calm blueprints also
allow you to provision

00:34:16.010 --> 00:34:18.800
and manage Kubernetes
through a control plane,

00:34:18.800 --> 00:34:22.560
through a one-click experience.

00:34:22.560 --> 00:34:25.186
Now, that's a little bit
about homogenizing consumption

00:34:25.186 --> 00:34:26.310
and operational interfaces.

00:34:26.310 --> 00:34:28.790
And that's what Calm
enables you to do.

00:34:28.790 --> 00:34:32.550
But a true hybrid stack goes
beyond just consumption.

00:34:32.550 --> 00:34:34.909
And where the need is to
ensure that all aspects

00:34:34.909 --> 00:34:38.600
of your workload stay consistent
on both sides, including things

00:34:38.600 --> 00:34:41.449
like performance, scale,
availability, security, data

00:34:41.449 --> 00:34:44.250
protection, things
of that nature,

00:34:44.250 --> 00:34:47.989
this calls for a different
level of building hybrid.

00:34:47.989 --> 00:34:50.900
And by that I mean it's
the same platform that

00:34:50.900 --> 00:34:53.480
needs to extend between
your on-premises environment

00:34:53.480 --> 00:34:55.520
and the target public cloud.

00:34:55.520 --> 00:34:59.120
And that's where our next
offering, which we call

00:34:59.120 --> 00:35:01.240
Xi Cloud Services accomplishes.

00:35:01.240 --> 00:35:02.870
That's exactly what it does.

00:35:02.870 --> 00:35:09.340
Think of it as a native
extension of our enterprise

00:35:09.340 --> 00:35:11.570
cloud platform into
the public cloud,

00:35:11.570 --> 00:35:14.360
into various public
clouds, including GCP.

00:35:14.360 --> 00:35:16.330
And it's our technology stack.

00:35:16.330 --> 00:35:17.810
It's the same
software technology

00:35:17.810 --> 00:35:20.480
stack that Nutanix has built
that we deliver on-premises now

00:35:20.480 --> 00:35:23.670
is delivered as a service in
all in various public cloud,

00:35:23.670 --> 00:35:26.460
including data centers
that we run and operate,

00:35:26.460 --> 00:35:28.610
but also within the GCP regions.

00:35:28.610 --> 00:35:31.010
With Xi, not only
do you maintain

00:35:31.010 --> 00:35:32.420
operational
consistency as you're

00:35:32.420 --> 00:35:33.836
expanding to public
cloud, but you

00:35:33.836 --> 00:35:38.150
retain all the applications
SLAs and the security

00:35:38.150 --> 00:35:39.800
model, and performance
characteristics,

00:35:39.800 --> 00:35:41.450
and everything
else that you would

00:35:41.450 --> 00:35:45.920
have carefully designed as you
built and run your application.

00:35:45.920 --> 00:35:48.740
And you could do that only
because it's the same platform

00:35:48.740 --> 00:35:50.090
on both sides.

00:35:50.090 --> 00:35:52.610
And the first service that
we are offering with Xi

00:35:52.610 --> 00:35:55.470
is disaster recovery
as a service.

00:35:55.470 --> 00:35:58.370
And we plan to expand
the set of use cases

00:35:58.370 --> 00:36:01.790
to encompass other
hybrid scenarios as well.

00:36:01.790 --> 00:36:03.860
And specifically
with Google, we're

00:36:03.860 --> 00:36:09.050
offering Xi services within
a GCP region colocated

00:36:09.050 --> 00:36:12.270
with low latency access to
the rest of the GCP services.

00:36:12.270 --> 00:36:14.540
So if you're a
customer who is looking

00:36:14.540 --> 00:36:16.160
to transition to
the public cloud

00:36:16.160 --> 00:36:17.860
for either your
existing workloads

00:36:17.860 --> 00:36:20.360
or to build new
workloads, you can

00:36:20.360 --> 00:36:22.970
leverage a combination
of Nutanix services

00:36:22.970 --> 00:36:25.040
and GCP services through
this joint offering.

00:36:30.060 --> 00:36:32.370
Now in addition to proximity,
there's a few more things

00:36:32.370 --> 00:36:35.430
we're doing on the
interface consumption side

00:36:35.430 --> 00:36:37.530
to make the whole
experience seamless

00:36:37.530 --> 00:36:38.880
for our joint customers.

00:36:38.880 --> 00:36:43.040
I'll talk through
some of those--

00:36:43.040 --> 00:36:46.360
yeah, as I get the right slide.

00:36:46.360 --> 00:36:48.310
A lot of this is the
platform integration

00:36:48.310 --> 00:36:50.450
so to enable and ensure
a seamless experience.

00:36:50.450 --> 00:36:53.137
So firstly, it's going to
be an integrated portal.

00:36:53.137 --> 00:36:54.970
There's a common portal
from which customers

00:36:54.970 --> 00:36:59.170
can consume a combination
of Nutanix services

00:36:59.170 --> 00:37:03.640
and a select set of GCP services
that we will make accessible

00:37:03.640 --> 00:37:04.570
through this portal.

00:37:04.570 --> 00:37:07.060
And those select
set would be the set

00:37:07.060 --> 00:37:08.770
of services that
would make sense

00:37:08.770 --> 00:37:12.880
to consume from within Xi,
things like BigData, BigQuery,

00:37:12.880 --> 00:37:16.520
that can actually act
on your data that's

00:37:16.520 --> 00:37:20.891
residing within the Xi
Cloud Services anyway.

00:37:20.891 --> 00:37:22.890
Another common problem
that customers experience

00:37:22.890 --> 00:37:24.180
is management of credentials.

00:37:24.180 --> 00:37:26.190
And through a single
sign-on experience,

00:37:26.190 --> 00:37:29.374
we allow you to maintain
a common identity

00:37:29.374 --> 00:37:31.790
to access a combination of,
again, Xi and Nutanix services

00:37:31.790 --> 00:37:33.776
so that you don't
really have to manage

00:37:33.776 --> 00:37:36.150
a different set of credentials
for each of your employees

00:37:36.150 --> 00:37:39.360
or operators who are
managing and administering

00:37:39.360 --> 00:37:43.550
these resources.

00:37:43.550 --> 00:37:44.990
We consolidate bills.

00:37:44.990 --> 00:37:47.210
And you get a single
bill from Nutanix

00:37:47.210 --> 00:37:50.540
that captures both
charges related to GCP

00:37:50.540 --> 00:37:54.072
and charges related to
the Nutanix Xi services.

00:37:54.072 --> 00:37:56.030
So you're not dealing
with two payment systems,

00:37:56.030 --> 00:38:01.340
and two ways of
tracking your bills,

00:38:01.340 --> 00:38:04.024
and things of that nature.

00:38:04.024 --> 00:38:06.440
And finally, this is important,
and Matt touched upon this

00:38:06.440 --> 00:38:08.499
before also, which is
network connectivity.

00:38:08.499 --> 00:38:10.040
And establishing
network connectivity

00:38:10.040 --> 00:38:13.440
is really usually very
cumbersome and painful.

00:38:13.440 --> 00:38:15.620
We unified that in
the sense that you

00:38:15.620 --> 00:38:18.020
could use a single
network connection

00:38:18.020 --> 00:38:21.770
to access a combination of both
Xi services and GCP services.

00:38:21.770 --> 00:38:24.380
And that connection
could be a VPN connection

00:38:24.380 --> 00:38:28.880
over the internet, or it could
be a GCP interconnect as a way

00:38:28.880 --> 00:38:32.490
to reach a combination of
GCP services and Xi services.

00:38:32.490 --> 00:38:36.330
And if you see all of these
capabilities together,

00:38:36.330 --> 00:38:40.790
the goal is to enable a more
seamless, simplified experience

00:38:40.790 --> 00:38:46.310
to access what we call a
set of hybrid services.

00:38:46.310 --> 00:38:50.180
So I spoke a lot
about essentially

00:38:50.180 --> 00:38:53.240
various capabilities,
and mission statements,

00:38:53.240 --> 00:38:54.530
and use cases, and so forth.

00:38:54.530 --> 00:38:58.520
Let's bring all of it together
using an actual customer

00:38:58.520 --> 00:38:59.700
example.

00:38:59.700 --> 00:39:02.690
Here's a real
scenario where there's

00:39:02.690 --> 00:39:06.440
an existing joint customer
of Nutanix and GCP.

00:39:06.440 --> 00:39:09.200
And they were interested
in essentially procuring

00:39:09.200 --> 00:39:12.080
excess compute capacity
on the fly, on demand,

00:39:12.080 --> 00:39:16.439
as and when their
application needs it.

00:39:16.439 --> 00:39:18.230
So during peak seasons,
it's an application

00:39:18.230 --> 00:39:20.220
that serves both internal
and external users

00:39:20.220 --> 00:39:22.130
for that organization.

00:39:22.130 --> 00:39:23.990
And during peak
season, they need

00:39:23.990 --> 00:39:26.970
capacity for that application
to expand on demand.

00:39:26.970 --> 00:39:28.880
It's a pretty standard
web application

00:39:28.880 --> 00:39:31.190
that consists of
Apache web servers,

00:39:31.190 --> 00:39:35.540
and Oracle databases, and
JBOSS application servers.

00:39:35.540 --> 00:39:38.090
And primarily they wanted
the web server capacity

00:39:38.090 --> 00:39:41.100
to expand on demand
without human intervention,

00:39:41.100 --> 00:39:46.910
and without any lead times, and
without overprocuring capacity.

00:39:46.910 --> 00:39:50.690
Now, it's a pretty standard
cloud bursting use case.

00:39:50.690 --> 00:39:53.690
And I'm sure many of you've
heard of this case before.

00:39:53.690 --> 00:39:55.280
But it's pretty
hard to accomplish

00:39:55.280 --> 00:39:58.340
without the right
degree of automation

00:39:58.340 --> 00:40:00.780
and without having to
retransform or change

00:40:00.780 --> 00:40:02.787
the nature in which
your application is run,

00:40:02.787 --> 00:40:04.370
especially if you're
looking to access

00:40:04.370 --> 00:40:06.080
the public cloud
as a way to procure

00:40:06.080 --> 00:40:08.420
that additional capacity.

00:40:08.420 --> 00:40:10.940
So we've implemented
or are in the process

00:40:10.940 --> 00:40:14.270
of implementing a solution
for this customer that

00:40:14.270 --> 00:40:17.810
includes a lot of the
components that we spoke about.

00:40:17.810 --> 00:40:21.050
The Calm acts as the
overarching orchestration layer.

00:40:21.050 --> 00:40:24.740
And it's constantly monitoring
performance and utilization

00:40:24.740 --> 00:40:26.180
of the application.

00:40:26.180 --> 00:40:32.480
And it's built to orchestrate
new web VMs in GCP.

00:40:32.480 --> 00:40:35.680
This is actually
within Xi in GCP.

00:40:35.680 --> 00:40:38.870
And therefore, if you need
more capacity on the web

00:40:38.870 --> 00:40:41.750
without any kind of operator
intervention, the system

00:40:41.750 --> 00:40:43.580
itself detects that,
OK, it's reached

00:40:43.580 --> 00:40:45.500
a threshold, at
which point it needs

00:40:45.500 --> 00:40:47.060
to spin up these instances.

00:40:47.060 --> 00:40:49.340
And it launches them,
adapts them to the web farm.

00:40:49.340 --> 00:40:51.420
It does other forms
of automation,

00:40:51.420 --> 00:40:53.270
things like assigning
IP addresses,

00:40:53.270 --> 00:40:56.360
building connectivity back to
the on-premises environment

00:40:56.360 --> 00:40:59.840
where the actual database
runs, and modifying DNS records

00:40:59.840 --> 00:41:02.930
and things of that nature,
all the automation tasks

00:41:02.930 --> 00:41:05.660
that you would
otherwise manually do.

00:41:05.660 --> 00:41:07.940
And we leverage,
as I was saying,

00:41:07.940 --> 00:41:11.570
that common network connectivity
between GCP resources

00:41:11.570 --> 00:41:15.170
and Xi resources as a way
to establish connectivity

00:41:15.170 --> 00:41:17.884
between on-premises and
the cloud environment.

00:41:17.884 --> 00:41:18.800
And there you have it.

00:41:18.800 --> 00:41:22.730
Essentially, this is how we've
solved this customer's use

00:41:22.730 --> 00:41:23.314
case.

00:41:23.314 --> 00:41:24.980
And if you see, it's
because of the fact

00:41:24.980 --> 00:41:29.860
that Xi is running inside GCP
that this automation has become

00:41:29.860 --> 00:41:33.290
amendable in the sense that the
customer's workloads need not

00:41:33.290 --> 00:41:35.240
have been transformed
in any way.

00:41:35.240 --> 00:41:37.520
There has been no redesign
of their applications.

00:41:37.520 --> 00:41:40.554
The way they've constructed
their virtual machines

00:41:40.554 --> 00:41:42.470
and the way they've
designed and characterized

00:41:42.470 --> 00:41:44.150
performance for
their applications

00:41:44.150 --> 00:41:47.960
on their on-premises platform,
all of those characteristics

00:41:47.960 --> 00:41:53.550
are retained as these instances
burst into the public cloud.

00:41:53.550 --> 00:41:55.550
It's a pretty simple
example, but it illustrates

00:41:55.550 --> 00:41:57.380
essentially the power
of the joint solution

00:41:57.380 --> 00:41:59.990
because it addresses
things like automation

00:41:59.990 --> 00:42:04.730
and seamless expansion without
the need for refactoring

00:42:04.730 --> 00:42:06.110
your applications.

00:42:06.110 --> 00:42:08.630
And the benefits to customers
is really the simplicity

00:42:08.630 --> 00:42:11.090
that it brings to the table
and the economic advantage

00:42:11.090 --> 00:42:15.160
of adding capacity
only when you need it,

00:42:15.160 --> 00:42:17.030
guaranteed availability.

00:42:17.030 --> 00:42:19.410
This is a pretty business
critical application.

00:42:19.410 --> 00:42:22.370
And therefore, guaranteeing
availability and performance

00:42:22.370 --> 00:42:24.800
SLAs for this
application while you're

00:42:24.800 --> 00:42:28.520
taking advantage of the
economic elasticity is critical.

00:42:28.520 --> 00:42:30.412
And it also illustrates
the way in which

00:42:30.412 --> 00:42:31.370
workloads can be moved.

00:42:31.370 --> 00:42:33.030
I showed you a cloud
bursting use case.

00:42:33.030 --> 00:42:35.210
You can clearly see how
this can be extended over

00:42:35.210 --> 00:42:37.994
to a mobility use case
where instead of bursting

00:42:37.994 --> 00:42:39.410
into the cloud,
you could actually

00:42:39.410 --> 00:42:43.060
move that entire application
over into the cloud using

00:42:43.060 --> 00:42:45.220
exactly the same constructs
and the same tools

00:42:45.220 --> 00:42:48.100
that we built to
address this use case.

00:42:48.100 --> 00:42:50.820
Anyway, so hopefully
that gives you

00:42:50.820 --> 00:42:52.570
a sense for the why,
and the what, and the

00:42:52.570 --> 00:42:56.380
how, along with the
actual real example

00:42:56.380 --> 00:42:58.690
that illustrates what
we're bringing together

00:42:58.690 --> 00:43:01.300
between Nutanix and Google.

00:43:01.300 --> 00:43:03.910
So I will be around
in the Nutanix

00:43:03.910 --> 00:43:05.110
booth in the conference.

00:43:05.110 --> 00:43:06.940
So if you have any
